<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <!-- The division picks its own culture; invariant mode would break time zones and collation. -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>false</InvariantGlobalization>
    <!-- Required for IDE0005 (unused usings) to run at build time; CS1591 stays suppressed. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
    <RootNamespace>$(MSBuildProjectName)</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>$(MSBuildProjectName)</AssemblyName>
    <!-- In CI the assembly carries no absolute path of the runner, so the same commit builds the same
         binary wherever it is built. -->
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      THE VERSION OF THE SITE, and the three rules that give it a meaning.

      A number promises a rule, and a version people believe while it says nothing is worse than no
      version at all. So this is the rule, and it holds for the number below:

        PATCH  x.y.Z  fixes only: no migration, no new page or section.
        MINOR  x.Y.0  new features, and/or ADDITIVE migrations.
        MAJOR  X.0.0  the package cannot be delivered by FTP alone: the database has to be replaced,
                      or the new code cannot read the data already in production.

      ⚠️ MAJOR is not a judgement of importance. It is the answer to one question, "is FTP enough, or
      does the database have to change too?", which is the only one that turns a delivery into
      something to arrange with whoever runs the server.

      ⚠️ THE CONTRACT HALF OF AN EXPAND/CONTRACT IS MINOR. The migration that drops a column no code
      has used since the previous release is not additive, but FTP is enough for it: MAJOR would say
      otherwise. It is delivered with open eyes, though: the delivery sheet says so in red and asks
      for a fresh copy of the database before the upload (docs/DELIVERING.md; note
      2026-09-27-il-contract-e-minor).

      ⚠️ THE NUMBER LIVES HERE, NOT ON THE COMMAND LINE. It is raised in a commit, so that whoever
      reviews that commit sees the promise next to what justifies it. `release.yml` refuses a tag that
      is not exactly "v" followed by this number: the tag names the release, it never renames the
      package.

      ⚠️ The number is not the whole stamp: the site shows "0.2.0 · <commit>". The number is the name
      we give a build; the commit is the only thing that says which code it is, and two builds under
      the same number can be two different codes. The SDK writes the commit after the "+" of the
      informational version (Source Link, from the .git of the checkout), `BuildInfo` reads it from
      there, and `/api/version`, `diagnostics/startup.txt` and the footer of every page show it.

      Its shape is kept by a test (`BuildInfoTests`): three numbers separated by dots, no "v" in front,
      no suffix.
    -->
    <Version>0.6.0</Version>
    <!-- The default, written out because `BuildInfo` depends on it: without it the commit is gone. -->
    <IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>true</IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(IsTestProject)' == 'true'">
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
